                              --------------
                              Major Features
                              --------------

Heres a list of TUCFGII's major features:

  All compiled C code. Extremely fast. A speed comparison on my 030:
  Of 800 Files, in 25 UDbases. 

    Standard Arexx: 1+  Minute    (TUCFG I  v0.0-0.99)
    Compiled Arexx: 30+ Seconds   (TUCFG I  v1.0-1.99)
    Compiled C    : 6-7 Seconds   (TUCFG II v1.0+)

  NFC... "No F'ing Config"  ALL program options controlled
  through the CLI. (Aren't you sick of config files ?)
	  
  For files: 
  ~~~~~~~~~~
  Supports ALL Item attributes like: Free Download, Sysop Favorite,
  Private upload, Failed CRC, Missing, Unfinished, Validated, Killed,
  Please Kill (during amaint), # Times D/l'd, 80+ character descriptions.
  Items can be listed in oldest to newest files or alphabetical. 
  Items such as Failed CRC, Sysop Fav, and Free are marked with text
  stating such.

  For UDbase Dirs:
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  Supports CNets Access Level, and Access flag(s).
  Also supports flags such as closed, invitation only, 
  and forced private.  Will also optionally alphabetically
  sort dirs. Suggested method, until I fix to work like CNets.

  Supports a main header and footer text. And each UDbase
  may have its own custom header text.

  SUPER EASY to install and use. 


                               ------------
                               Installation
                               ------------

Figure out which version you need. TUCFGII.sml supports 75 UDbases
and 500 files per. TUCFGII.big supports 150 UDbases, and 999 files
per Udbase. Delete the one you DON'T need, and rename TUCFGII.??? If
you want.

Copy TUCFGII to anywhere you want. C: would be a good place. Thats 
about it. I've included a script (tucfgii.exe) which I use to make my
lists. I run this script from a BBSEVENT. Or I just execute it manually
with the AmigaDOS execute command.

Note:
If your running the Arexx version you should delete everything related 
to it. Including the RPhi, RPStart, and the Rexxplslib.library. These 
are no longer needed, and are just taking up space. (Don't forget to
re-add rexxmast to your startup-sequence).

Note 2: 
If any UDbase access levels are set to include the Access 31 it will
freak this program out. It wont crash, but dirs with those flags
set for access levels will not list. So your best off not to
use anything above 23 until CNet fully supports 24 and greater.
If your confused logon, go to a udbase and use the EL command,
check the access vars section, and "Access Groups" should be
any value up to 23. CNet will allow 0-31 in there, but will actually
only support 0-23. Or you can just run the program. If it seems to
freak and skip dirs etc., you've got something wrong. This could also
be considered a feature. Think about it!

Note 3:
Turn off all upload and download access's for message bases.
This program will skip a 'base' if no one can download from it,
so make sure all your MESSAGE ONLY BASES do not allow ANY access
levels to download.

                              -------------
                              Using TUCFGII
                              -------------

TUCFGII is to be run from the CLI only. Atleast ONE
option flag must be passed to the program. It doesn't
matter what. I usually atleast pass the access level.
Think of this program as a user on-line your bbs. Give
the program an Access level, AND/OR User UDbase flags.
And other misc. options.

Following is descriptions of TUCFGII's commands:

  -A#  :Access, A CNet access level. No ranges are supported.
		Enter a number between 0 and 23.

  -c$  :Config file location/name. I.e. "-cs:tucfgii.cfg


  -d#  :Days, The number of days to use as a comparison for
        highlighting new files. Any number between 1 and 32000.

  -F#  :Flag, A CNet access flag(s).You may supply as many as wanted.
        But each flag must be defined separately. (See examples below)
		Any number between 0 and 23.

  -f$  :Footer text location and name.

  -h$  :Header text location and name.
 
  -I   :Include "Invitation" subboards when making list.

  -P   :Include "Force Private" subboards when making list.
        This flag will also include private uploads.

  -R   :Re-Comment actual files on drives. This feature will take the
        on-line short 80 char description, and actually comment the file.
        This comment can be viewed with the DOS LIST command. Or from 
        programs like diskmaster, etc. You might want to run this option
        once every other month, depending on usage of your bbs, etc.     

  -s   :Alphabetically sort Files.

  -S   :Alphabetically sort UDBases.

  -t#  :Show top # downloaded files in each section.

  -T#  :ONLY Show top # downloaded files in each section.

  -u   :Force first letter of filename to uppercase.

  -U   :Force all filenames to uppercase.

  -V   :Include unvalidated files.

  -w   :Suppress printing of short description, and the Sysop Favorite,
        Free D/L, and Failed CRC Flags.

  -W   :Suppress printing of Sysop Favorite, Free D/L, and Failed CRC Flags.

  -Z   :Zoom mode. Specify this flag if you ONLY want dirs
        with access flags to be listed. This means you must
        also supply the -F flag(s).


In above options:  # means a number is expected. 
				   $ means text is expected.

Some Examples:

TUCFGII -A2                <--Show all files an access level 2 would see

TUCFGII -S                 <--Show all files a newuser would see (sort udbases)

TUCFGII -s                 <--Show all files a newuser would see (sort files)

TUCFGII -A2 -F13	       <--Same as above with udbase flag 13 set.

TUCFGII -A2 -F13 -d10      <--display New in last 10 days marked new

TUCFGII -A23 -F23 -F22     <-- now will check for those TWO flags.

TUCFGII -A2 -d 10          <-INCORRECT, NO SPACE between -D and 10!!
TUCFGII -A2 -d10           <-Correct

TUCFGII -A2 -d15 -hsystext:header -fsystext:footer

TUCFGII -A2 -F2 -Z -d15 -t10 -s -S -P -I -W -w

Try a few different options. And once your comfortable with
its options your ready to have it create lists.

The program uses redirection to actually create lists.
For example lets say you want to have a listing go to
RAM: under the title "My_Files". 

TUCFGII >ram:My_Files -A2 -d10 
        ^
 Notice the ">" (greater than) symbol. This tells TUCFGII to send all
of its output to a file. You may also use ">>" to APPEND output to 
another file. For more information on redirection see your Amiga Dos
Manual. 

TUCFGII does not waste a bunch of code checking YOUR input too much. 
So please be careful.

For a file section to be listed, TUCFGII must match its
access level, download access, and access flags. (With no config file)

To have TUCFGII display a custom 'header' text for each UD section,
copy a text file into the udbase's DATA dir. For example I have
a section called New_Uploads. So in "UDBASE0:NEW_UPLOADS/DATA/"
I copy a file there named "header". 


